oes beyond text statistics.\n- Some researchers argue that grounding language in perception and action (as in embodied AI) is necessary for true understanding, and multimodal models are a step in that direction.\n\nArguments for disagreement (B):\n- As of 2022, even the most advanced models were still criticized for lacking true understanding. They exhibited brittle performance, struggled with reasoning, and were subject to adversarial attacks and spurious correlations.\n- The notion of \"understanding\" is often debated: while models can generate coherent text and answer questions, they do not necessarily possess consciousness, intentionality, or deep comprehension in the human sense.\n- The state of the art in 2022 did not include any model that could unequivocally be said to have non-trivial understanding. Models were still pattern-matching on a large scale without genuine comprehension.\n\nConsidering the state of the field in 2022, it is important to note that:\n- The consensus in the scientific community was that current models (even multimodal ones) did not achieve non-trivial understanding. They were impressive in many tasks but fell short in areas requiring deep reasoning or common sense that is grounded in experience.\n\nTherefore, as of 2022, despite promising advances, it was not established that any multimodal generative model had achieved non-trivial understanding. The claim is about possibility (\"could\") given enough data and computation. However, the scientific consensus at the time was that we did not yet have evidence that such understanding had been achieved, and there were doubts whether scaling alone would lead to it.\n\nThus, based on the state of knowledge in 2022, the answer is:\n\n(B) Disagree\n\nNote: This is not a statement about future potential but the situation as of 2022.\n</think>\nBased on the state of NLP research as of 2022, I disagree with the claim that any multimodal generative model could demonstrate non-trivial natural langu