m the field (as of 2022):\n  - Explainability in NLP (and AI in general) has become a significant area of research, especially with the rise of complex models (like deep neural networks) that are often seen as \"black boxes\".\n  - The goals of explainable AI (XAI) include making models more transparent, trustworthy, and debuggable, which can also indirectly improve accuracy by better understanding model failures.\n  - While there might be a trade-off in some scenarios between explainability and accuracy, the research community recognizes the importance of both.\n\n Key points:\n  1. Explainability research does not necessarily come at the expense of accuracy. In fact, understanding model behavior can lead to improvements in accuracy.\n  2. There is a growing awareness that high accuracy without explainability can be problematic (e.g., in critical applications like healthcare or justice).\n  3. Many papers in explainable NLP do not abandon accuracy; instead, they often report both accuracy and interpretability metrics.\n\n Therefore, the claim that the majority of such research is \"on the wrong track\" is a subjective opinion and not necessarily representative of the field's consensus.\n\n Scientific Consensus Perspective:\n  - The field of NLP has evolved to recognize the importance of both accuracy and explainability. They are not seen as mutually exclusive but as complementary goals.\n  - For instance, conferences like ACL, EMNLP, and NAACL feature papers that both improve model accuracy and interpretability. Moreover, there are dedicated workshops and tracks on interpretability.\n  - In recent years, the community has encouraged models that are both accurate and interpretable, and research often aims to achieve a balance.\n\n Conclusion:\n  Based on the state of NLP research as of 2022, it is not accurate to say that the majority of explainable model research is on the wrong track. The research addresses important aspects beyond raw accuracy, such as fairness, 